<section class="phero"><div class="phero-media"><img src="/assets/img/photos/lewiston-street.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/lewiston-street-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/lewiston-street.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="800" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/towns-we-cover/">Towns we cover</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Lewiston</span></nav><p class="kicker">Western Mountains &amp; Lakes · Androscoggin County</p><h1>Insurance Appraiser in Lewiston, Maine</h1><p class="lede">Lewiston’s multi-family tenements and mill buildings turn a single burst pipe or puffback into a multi-unit claim. We review carrier estimates and serve as appraiser when the amount of loss is in dispute.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-coral" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn btn-line light" href="/reach-us/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="spec"><div><dt>County</dt><dd>Androscoggin County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Western Mountains &amp; Lakes</dd></div><div><dt>Inspections</dt><dd>On site</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="snap"><h2>Lewiston at a glance</h2><dl>
<div><dt>County</dt><dd>Androscoggin County</dd></div><div><dt>Region</dt><dd>Western Mountains &amp; Lakes</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Neighborhoods and villages</dt><dd><ul class="chips"><li>Tree Streets</li><li>Little Canada</li><li>Bates Mill District</li><li>Lisbon Street downtown</li><li>South Lewiston</li><li>Auburn</li></ul></dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Housing mix</dt><dd>Wood-frame triple-deckers and multi-family tenements in the Tree Streets and downtown, brick mill buildings converted to offices and apartments, and capes, ranches and split-levels toward the city’s edges.</dd></div>
<div class="wide"><dt>Common claim risks</dt><dd><ul class="chips coral"><li>Burst pipes</li><li>Ice dams</li><li>Oil-burner puffback</li><li>Fallen trees</li></ul></dd></div></dl>
<h3>Storms on record</h3><ol class="mini-storms"><li><b>Dec 2023</b><span><strong>December 18 storm.</strong> Wind and heavy rain sent the Androscoggin to major flood levels; trees and lines down across the city.</span></li><li><b>Jan 1998</b><span><strong>Ice Storm of 1998.</strong> Heavy glaze toppled trees and lines across central and southern Maine; long outages in Androscoggin County.</span></li><li><b>Apr 1987</b><span><strong>April 1987 floods.</strong> Snowmelt and rain pushed the Androscoggin to one of its highest crests at Lewiston and Auburn.</span></li></ol></div>
<h2>A city of shared walls</h2>
<p>Lewiston grew around the Great Falls of the Androscoggin, and its housing still shows it. The Tree Streets and the old Little Canada area are dense with wood-frame triple-deckers and tenement blocks built for mill workers. When a pipe freezes on the third floor of one of those buildings in January, the water does not stop at one unit. Ceilings, wiring, flooring and tenant contents below all become part of the same claim, and that is where carrier estimates tend to come up short.</p>
<p>An insurance appraiser in Lewiston spends a lot of time on these multi-unit losses: following the water path, counting every affected room, and making sure loss-of-rents and code items that apply to rental buildings are not left out when the policy covers them.</p>
<h2>Mills, brick and the cost of old buildings</h2>
<p>The Bates Mill complex and other brick mills along the canals have been converted to offices, restaurants and apartments. Those buildings have heavy timber framing, tall masonry walls and large roof areas, and their repair costs do not resemble a standard Androscoggin County house. Commercial owners and associations in these buildings should expect the estimate fight to center on scope and access, not unit prices alone. Our <a href="/appraisal-work/commercial-condo-association-appraisal/">commercial and condo appraisal</a> page explains how appraisal works for larger buildings.</p>
<h3>Heating oil and puffback</h3>
<p>Like much of Maine, a large share of Lewiston’s older housing heats with oil. A burner misfire can blow soot through every room and every unit served by the system. Puffback claims are often underpaid because the soot is fine and spread wide, so cleaning scopes miss closets, ductwork and contents. See our <a href="/owner-guides/oil-burner-puffback-documentation/">puffback documentation guide</a> before cleanup starts.</p>
<h2>Storms that shaped local claims</h2>
<p>The December 18, 2023 storm combined damaging wind with heavy rain on snowpack, and the Androscoggin rose to major flood levels through Lewiston and Auburn. River flooding is excluded under homeowners policies, but the fallen trees and roof damage from the same storm were property claims. The April 1987 floods are still the reference point for older residents along the river, and the Ice Storm of January 1998 left much of the region without power for days, with frozen pipes following in homes that lost heat.</p>
<h2>What we check on a Lewiston inspection</h2>
<ul>
<li>Every unit and common area touched by water, not just the source unit.</li>
<li>Plaster, wood lath and old wiring that has to be opened up and brought to code.</li>
<li>Soot migration through shared heating systems and stairwells.</li>
<li>Tree removal and crane access on tight lots in South Lewiston and downtown.</li>
</ul>
<p>A Lewiston insurance appraiser can only resolve the amount of loss. If the carrier is disputing coverage or you are close to a policy deadline, talk to a Maine attorney.</p>

<h2>Appraisal work for Lewiston properties</h2>
<p>Every Maine Insurance Appraiser service is available in Lewiston and the surrounding towns, including:</p>
<ul><li><a href="/appraisal-work/homeowner-dispute-appraisal/">Homeowner Claim Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/noreaster-hurricane-appraisal/">Nor’easter &amp; Hurricane Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/water-damage-loss-appraisal/">Water Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/ice-dam-snow-load-appraisal/">Ice Dam &amp; Snow Load Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/fallen-tree-limb-appraisal/">Fallen Tree Damage Appraisal</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-work/carrier-estimate-line-review/">Insurance Estimate Review</a></li></ul>
<h2>Lewiston questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Does appraisal work for a Lewiston rental building with several units?</summary><p>Yes. Appraisal decides the amount of loss for the whole covered claim, which can include damage across several units, common areas and loss of rents if your policy covers it.</p></details><details><summary>The carrier’s puffback estimate only covers the basement. Is that normal?</summary><p>It is a common shortfall. Soot from an oil burner misfire often travels through ducts and stairwells, so the cleaning scope should be based on where soot is actually found throughout the building.</p></details><details><summary>Was the December 2023 Androscoggin flooding covered by homeowners policies?</summary><p>River flooding is generally excluded and falls under flood insurance. Wind damage and fallen trees from the same storm are typically handled under the property policy, so check your policy language.</p></details></div>
